# Plan 11a / D14 — the production edge.
#
# Three jobs: terminate TLS on the real hostname, serve the built SPA, and reverse-proxy the API
# path prefixes (including the WebSocket upgrade) to the api container.
#
# THE UPGRADE NEEDS NOTHING SPECIAL. Spike question E measured it rather than assuming it: a bare
# `reverse_proxy` produced HTTP 101 with a `sec-websocket-accept` header, the gateway then ran its
# own auth protocol over the proxied socket (auth_timeout frame, close 4001), a direct-connection
# control behaved byte-identically, and a wrong-path upgrade came back 502. No `header_up`, no
# transport block, no websocket matcher.
#
# TLS: automatic. Caddy provisions and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate for the site address
# below over ACME, and — because the site is named by hostname with automatic HTTPS on — Caddy
# also serves the HTTP→HTTPS redirect on :80 itself. There is deliberately NO explicit `http://`
# site block: one would take :80 away from Caddy's own automatic redirect and ACME handling for
# no gain. The redirect is proved by drill (308), not asserted here.
#
# There is NO auth gate in front of the app. Plan 02's auth is the gate; an IP allowlist is a
# Decisions-§ option the owner can turn on later in one directive.
#
# D4/GC1 portability: on on-prem metal this file is RE-POINTED (a different hostname, or an
# internal CA) and never rewritten.
{
	# HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 only. Left at the default, Caddy also opens HTTP/3 on 443/UDP and
	# advertises it with `alt-svc: h3=":443"; ma=2592000` — measured on the first bring-up. The
	# compose file publishes 443/TCP only, matching a host firewall that admits 80/tcp and
	# 443/tcp, so that advertisement is a promise nothing can keep: clients cache it for 30 days
	# and race a QUIC connection that never arrives before falling back. Publishing the UDP port
	# and opening it is the other coherent answer; delete this block if you take it.
	servers {
		protocols h1 h2
	}
}

hmis.crkmch.com {
	encode zstd gzip

	# Security headers, applied to every response the site produces — proxied and static alike.
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy "same-origin"
		Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()"
		-Server
	}

	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# THE PARITY LINE — PLAN 11g / DD1. ONE PREFIX, AND THE ORIGIN NOW HAS ONE RULE:
	# `/api/*` IS THE API, EVERYTHING ELSE IS THE APPLICATION.
	#
	# This line used to list twelve prefixes mirrored from the SPA's dev proxy — /auth /patients
	# /approvals /workflow /health /opd /billing /alerts /admin /ops /tariff /ws — and every one
	# of them was ALSO a path apps/web/src/router.tsx declares a SCREEN on. This matcher is
	# path-only (no method, no Accept), and it is evaluated FIRST, so a browser asking for the
	# /admin/users SCREEN was handed the API's {"statusCode":401} and 15 of the 20 screens did not
	# load at all. That is the 2026-08-24 synthetic smoke test's D1, and it could not reproduce in
	# dev because the vite server resolved the same collision the other way.
	#
	# Still pinned by apps/core/test/caddyfile-parity.test.ts, which now also asserts that NO SPA
	# route falls inside the proxied space — the leg that would have caught it.
	# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	@api path /api*

	# `handle` blocks are mutually exclusive and evaluated in the order written, which is what
	# this site needs: without them Caddy's directive ordering would run `try_files` (a rewrite)
	# BEFORE `reverse_proxy`, rewrite /api/health to /index.html because no such file exists under
	# the static root, and quietly stop the api from ever being reached.
	handle @api {
		# The prefix is a fact about the ORIGIN, not about the API: it is stripped here so the
		# api container keeps its own path space (`@Controller("billing")`, the compose
		# healthcheck on /health, the Prometheus scrape, and ~30 supertest e2e suites all still
		# speak the unprefixed path). `uri` runs in the rewrite phase, before `reverse_proxy`.
		uri strip_prefix /api
		reverse_proxy api:3000
	}

	# The SPA. /srv is where the Dockerfile's `web` target puts the vite build (T1-1); the
	# index.html fallback is what makes client-side routing survive a hard refresh.
	handle {
		root * /srv
		try_files {path} /index.html
		file_server
	}
}
